--TEST--
a modifier run or an attribute group written in front of a body's `type` is `E0133`, in a class, an interface and an enum body alike — `rule:types/type-alias`
--FILE--
<?nvs
// `rule:types/type-alias`: a class-scoped alias is reachable wherever its
// owner's name is, so there is no visibility to write, and nothing after the
// checker ever sees the name, so an attribute has nothing to attach to. The
// parser therefore refuses the decoration and keeps the declaration, rather
// than parsing a modifier run and dropping it.

// The undecorated form first, so an alias that stops being accepted fails
// here rather than as a missing refusal.
class Accepted {
    type Id = int|string;
}

class AModifier {
    public type Id = int|string;
}

// A run is one refusal, not one per keyword.
class TwoModifiers {
    private static type Id = int|string;
}

// An interface body and an enum body are the same rule, not two more.
interface AnAttributeGroup {
    #[Deprecated]
    type Id = int|string;
}

enum InAnEnum: int {
    Red = 1,

    #[Deprecated]
    type Id = int|string;
}

// A modifier in an enum body reaches the member path, where only the token
// after the run says what follows it. The alias is a member the enum owns,
// so this is `E0133` alone and not a second "not a case" refusal.
enum AModifierInAnEnum: int {
    On = 1,

    public static type Id = int|string;
}

// Both runs at once are one refusal spanning both.
class Both {
    #[Deprecated]
    private type Id = int|string;
}

echo "unreachable\n";
--EXPECT--

--EXPECTF-ERROR--
error[E0133]: a `type` alias takes no modifier and no attribute group
   --> case.nvs:15:5
